  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 158 PM CDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\nSunday - Monday: Cooling Down with Some Rain Chances \n\nThe general synoptic pattern over the next couple of days keeps \nzonal flow aloft with a surface cold front passing through the \nregion during the overnight and into Sunday. As a result, \ntemperatures will be noticeably cooler for areas north of I-90 \nfor Sunday in lieu of the previously mentioned cold front. \nConsequently, the NBM keeps highs in the 50s and 60s for most \nlocations outside of portions of northeast IA and southwest WI \nwhere the aforementioned cold front has yet to push through with\nhighs reaching into the 70s. As we head later into the day on \nSunday and into Monday, a shortwave trough pushes through the \nGreat Plains with some increasing precipitation potential. Some \nsmall rain chances (10-20%) present themselves late Sunday \nevening and into the overnight as a weak baroclinic zone and \nlow-level frontogenetic axis may instigate some light \nprecipitation as suggested in the CAMs across north-central WI \nand southeast MN. However, the recent HREF keeps much of the \nprobabilities (10-40%) for measurable precipitation confined to \nnorth-central WI and southeast MN on Sunday. As we head into \nMonday, precipitation chances increase (10-40%) a bit more \nMonday evening and overnight again for north-central WI and \nsoutheast MN as the aforementioned shortwave trough closes in on\nthe region. However, recent guidance has continued to \ndemonstrate a northerly shift with the grand ensemble \n(GEFS/EC/Canadian ensemble) only having lower-end probabilities \n(10-40%) for rainfall amounts over 0.05\" in north-central WI \nand southeast MN. \n\nTuesday - Friday: Cooler But Still Above Normal with Additional Rain \nPotential \n\nBy the time Tuesday rolls around, the aforementioned shortwave \nimpulse pulls northward into Canada with precipitation chances \ndiminishing later into the day on Tuesday. However, right on the \nheels of the primary shortwave impulse is a secondary shortwave \ntrough that swings through the Northern Plains Tuesday night and \ninto Wednesday bringing additional precipitation chances along with \nit. Consequently, the grand ensemble (GEFS/EC/Canadian ensemble) has \nmuch higher probabilities (60-90%) for measurable rainfall by \nWednesday evening and into Thursday morning across the local area. \nHowever, given the fairly weak nature of the forcing, probabilities \nfor rainfall amounts over 0.1\" are fairly low (10-50%) during this \nperiod as well. Additional chances for rainfall will be present into \nnext weekend, however given the low predictability between \ndeterministic and ensemble guidance for how any shortwave \nimpulses may manifest during this timeframe, have lower \nconfidence in precipitation potential for Friday and into next \nweekend. In any case, not expecting much for impacts with any \nrainfall throughout the upcoming week. \n\nAs far as temperatures are concerned, cooler temperatures in the \nwake of the aforementioned cold front will give way to a warming \ntrend beginning on Tuesday. This is mostly due to upper-level \nridging building back into the region for the latter half of the \nwork week which in turn will allow for temperatures to warm with the \nNBM having a respectable shot (30-80% chance) for highs to return \ninto the 70s as soon as Wednesday for much of the area south of I-\n94. Additionally, with this broader upper-level ridging and \nzonal flow in place, really not expecting this air mass to \nchange much into next weekend which is suggest in high \ntemperatures fields in the NBM. Therefore, it seems fairly \nreasonable to conclude that we will trend warmer than normal \ninto Halloween.",
